This documents the "BIOS-level file system", which is not the same as the
file system used by the operating system. The files in this file system are
the disk partitions.

A directory entry consists of four fields (20 bytes):

* File ID (2 bytes): This uniquely identifies this file in this directory.

* Type (1 byte): Indicates the file type.

* Flags (1 byte): Flags with various functions (see below).

* Data (16 bytes): For direct files, this is the contents of the file. For
indirect files, it consists of two 64-bit sector numbers, being the start
and end sector of the file. If the number of sectors cannot fit in a 64-bit
number, then the virtual sector size is the usable disk capacity divided by
2 to the power of 64, and then rounded up to the sector size. (Note that it
is possible for indirect files to overlap, that files must be contiguous,
and that the length of a file must be either exactly sixteen bytes or a
multiple of the sector size.)

The meaning of the flags is:

* bit0 = Set for direct files, or clear for indirect files.

* bit1 = Read only.

* bit2 = In use.

* bit3 = The contents is a directory listing.

* bit4 = This bit is used for selective backups.

* bit5 = This bit is used for partition managers to tell it that the
contents of this file should not be moved.

* bit6 = Indicates that this is the default file.

* bit7 = Set if this file exists, or clear if it has been deleted or has
never existed.

File types can be:

* 0x00 = Plain data file, of an unspecified type.

* 0x01 = Subdirectory. (The flag bit3 also must be set, in this case.)

* 0x02 = Executable file that takes over the entire system, such as the
operating system kernel. (It usually also includes some configuration.)

* 0x03 = NVRAM backup.

* 0x04 = Executable file that can run with the ROM BASIC and without
taking up the entire system, so that it can later return to the BASIC.

* 0x05 = The file system partition for the full mode operating system,
which is described in a separate file.

* 0x06 = PC disk image.

* 0x07 = Swap area.

* 0x08 = RAID configuration.

* 0x09 = BASIC file.

* 0x0A = Directory of bad blocks. (Also set the flag bit3.)

* 0x0B = The contents of this file are bad blocks, and cannot contain any
usable data. This file should be indirect, because direct is not useful.

(The normal booting process would involve selecting the appropriate file
of type 0x02 (possibly configured in NVRAM or selected manually, or using
the default file), and loading its contents into RAM and then starting to
execute it from the beginning.)
